			<div class="content" id="post-content-4825499">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>A river within the middle east that erodes land around it at much faster rate attempting to form new branches to the river.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Is the erosion fast enough that like it's obviously anomalous? I'm just, trying to get a feel for a) how the foundation discovers this is anomalous b) what they do to contain it that causes the fish to attempt to escape containment</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and personal began behaving abnormally due to the mind altering effects of the fish in the water, and began requesting people around them drink more water or &quot;take a nice shower&quot; attempting to get personal to interact with water until fish were recontained</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why do the fish want people to interact with the water? Is this an attempt to murder 'em so the fish can escape? Or?</p>
<hr />
<p>My central concern here is that a lot of the narrative feels very&#8230;passive: there's a sequence of events that like, makes sense for it to follow but there's not really any active characters or intrigue or anything driving it forward; there's no real conflict created as a result because like, the Foundation aren't really doing anything to stop it, so it's just&#8230;happening?</p>
<p>Beyond this, the idea feels kinda reminiscent of a lot of generic memetic stuff like e.g. <a href="/scp-2790">SCP-2790</a> and I'm concerned the story you're telling isn't quite interesting enough to compensate.</p>
</div>
</div>
